They are entirely separate, the Canadian Government or the Department of Marine and Fisheries have absolutely nothing at all to do with the Imperial Munitions Board, so far as relates to our shipbuilding programme, and any ships that the latter has ordered are for Imperial account. The duty of the Imperial Munitions Board is to see that they are finished and paid for;
5 p.m. and the Canadian Government assumes no responsibility for any of the ships that are under construction at the present time for the Imperial Munitions Board.
I was about to say that one of the first difficulties that confronted me when considering the adoption of a permanent shipbuilding policy, was that of steel ship's plates. I think hon. gentlemen are aware that there is no duty on ship's plates coming into Canada. They are doubtless also aware that there has not been, and there is not at the present time, a rolling mill in Canada for the manufacture of these plates. If Canada is to become a shipbuilding nation, as I see no reason why she should not

I am pleased to advise the hon. member that they are compulsory at the present time. Every vessel of over 1,600 tons is compelled to have a qualified operator on board. The radiotelegraph school was formerly, as hon. members will no doubt know, at Halifax, but owing to the terrible catastrophe that happened there on. December 6 last, the 'headquarters were removed to Ottawa. All those operators are sworn in for the duration of the war as belonging to the Royal Canadian Navy. They are engaged first as learners, and then, if they pass all the examinations to become 23}
operators, they are sent to itlhe various stations throughout Canada. As regards the conference that took place at London, while I was present at the time in London, I am not familiar with all the details, but I shall be glad to give the details later on.
Mr. LEMilEUX: Some six years ago my successor in the Post Office Department, Hon. Mr. Pelletier, introduced a Bill in this House with a view to getting cheaper rates for the transmission of messages across the ocean. A^ contract was signed with a company having its own system of radio-telegraphy-!I forget the name of the company and the system. The contract was a most favourable one. Last year, at the end of the session, my hon. friend's predecessor, Hon. Mr. Hazen, introduced a Bill, giving to the Marconi Company the benefit of that contract, the contract being transferred to that company. Mr. Allan, of Montreal, as the minister knows very well, is the president of 'that company. Does the minister know whether the company has done anything?-(whether the contract has been adhered to? I was very pleased to see last year that the board of directors was under the chairmanship of my friendi Mr. Allan of Montreal, a well-known and highly respected citizen. I understand that this matter is under the direction of either the Marine Department or the Post Office Department, I am not sure which. Has anything been done in connection with that Marconi Company?
